.TH GCSL 1 2026-10-18 "Peios gcsl 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
gcsl \- run, check, and format GCSL programs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gcsl
.RI [ COMMAND ]
.br
.B gcsl run
.RI [ OPTIONS ] " FILE"
.br
.B gcsl check
.RB [ --message-format " human|json" ]
.RI [ PATH ...]
.br
.B gcsl fmt
.RB [ --check ]
.RI [ PATH ...]
.br
.B gcsl lsp
.SH DESCRIPTION
GCSL is a statically typed language for configuration and scripting.
The executable includes the standard library and language server.
Without a command, it starts an interactive read-evaluate-print loop (REPL).
.SH COMMANDS
.SS run
Check and execute FILE.
If FILE belongs to a workspace, check the whole workspace before running FILE and the files it depends on.
Otherwise, check and run FILE alone with the standard library.
An error anywhere in the checked workspace prevents execution.
.PP
Print the result to standard output, followed by a newline, unless it is the empty record {}.
Print a top-level string as its raw contents; other values use their GCSL representation.
Diagnostics and warnings go to standard error.
A requested dump stops execution after the last requested stage; it does not evaluate the program.
.SS check
Check files and workspaces without evaluating them.
A file argument checks its enclosing workspace, or that file alone if it is outside one.
A file reached through a path excluded from workspace discovery is checked alone.
A directory argument discovers workspaces at or below it, including nested workspaces, and also checks an enclosing workspace when the directory is inside one.
Each workspace is checked once.
Standalone source files under a directory are not checked unless explicitly named.
With no paths, check the workspace containing the working directory; outside a workspace, report an error.
.PP
Human-readable diagnostics go to standard error, followed by a summary.
Warnings alone do not make checking fail.
With JSON output, each diagnostic is a separate object on one line of standard output; the summary remains on standard error.
.SS fmt
Format named files in place.
For a directory, format all discovered .gcsl files, including standalone files and nested workspaces.
Hidden names and symbolic-link directories are excluded from traversal.
With no paths, format the enclosing workspace, excluding nested workspaces; outside a workspace, report an error.
The path \- reads standard input and writes formatted source to standard output; it cannot be combined with other paths.
.PP
A lexical or syntax error leaves that file unchanged and does not prevent formatting other files.
Type errors do not prevent formatting.
An unchanged file keeps its modification time.
Changed files are replaced through a newly created adjacent temporary file with the original permissions.
Temporary-name collisions are skipped without modifying the colliding file.
A symbolic-link file argument updates its target and preserves the link.
Line endings become LF.
.SS lsp
Serve the Language Server Protocol on standard input and standard output.
Logs go to standard error.
The server checks source and does not execute programs.
It provides diagnostics, hover, definitions, references, highlights, checked rename, document and workspace symbols, formatting, completion, signature help, inlay hints, and spelling quick fixes.
.PP
Positions use UTF-8 when the client offers it; otherwise they use UTF-16.
Rename does not rename files, directories, built-ins, or standard-library declarations.
Formatting uses GCSL's fixed style and ignores client formatting preferences.
Only file URIs with an empty or localhost authority and an absolute decoded path are mapped to local files.
Other document URIs are handled as standalone documents.
.PP
Send shutdown before exit for a clean protocol shutdown.
.SS REPL
Each nonempty input line is a block body in a shared session.
Declarations remain available to later inputs.
A value other than {} prints as "value : Type"; strings use quoted GCSL representation.
Errors are displayed and the session continues.
A failed input does not commit its new bindings, but external effects already performed are not undone.
Press Ctrl+C while entering a line to discard it and Ctrl+D to end input.
History lasts for the session only.
A prompt is shown only when standard input is a terminal; piped input receives no prompt.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -h , " --help"
Show help for the executable or selected command.
.TP
.BR -V , " --version"
Show the executable's version.
.TP
.B --dump-tokens
For run, print lexical tokens.
.TP
.B --dump-ast
For run, print the parsed syntax tree.
.TP
.B --dump-types
For run, print the syntax tree with inferred types.
.TP
.B --dump-ir
For run, print the lowered intermediate representation.
.TP
.B --message-format human|json
For check, select human-readable or newline-delimited JSON diagnostics.
The default is human.
.TP
.B --check
For fmt, write nothing and list files that would change on standard output.
For standard input, list <stdin> if it would change and do not print formatted source.
.SH DIAGNOSTIC FORMAT
Each JSON diagnostic has path, severity, message, line, column, end_line, end_column, labels, and notes fields.
Severity is error or warning.
Lines and columns are 1-based; columns count characters.
Each label has path, line, column, end_line, end_column, and text fields.
Notes are strings.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
Warnings alone are successful.
A REPL that reaches end of input succeeds even if individual inputs reported language errors.
.TP
.B 1
A program fails checking or evaluation; formatting finds invalid syntax; a formatting check would change a file; or the language server exits without shutdown.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command usage, an input/output failure, or failure to start the interpreter worker.
For check and fmt, an input/output failure takes precedence over language or formatting errors.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
A nonempty value disables colored diagnostics.
.TP
.B PATH
Used by process-execution built-ins when a program name has no slash.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Used by the host's temporary-directory selection for fs.temp_dir, unless an embedding host supplies a temporary root.
.TP
.B TERM
Used by terminal input handling.
.PP
Subprocesses inherit the host environment and working directory.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B workspace.gcsl
Workspace root marker and an ordinary source file.
The closest enclosing marker determines a file's workspace.
.TP
.B *.gcsl
GCSL source files.
Hidden names and nested workspace trees are excluded when reading one workspace.
.TP
.B <stdlib>/NAME.gcsl
Diagnostic name for an embedded standard module; not a required installed file.
.SH SECURITY
Executing GCSL is not sandboxed.
Programs can read and write files and start subprocesses with the host process's permissions.
Checking and language-server analysis do not run program effects.
Nesting limits do not bound execution time, total allocation, or captured process output.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check and run a standalone file:
.PP
.nf
    gcsl check hello.gcsl
    gcsl run hello.gcsl
.fi
.PP
Check formatting without rewriting a workspace:
.PP
.nf
    gcsl fmt --check
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gcsl (5),
.BR gcsl-stdlib (3)
